    "case_summary": "An 83-year-old woman with history of:- Recent non-ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction- Type 2 diabetes- Hypertension- Recent coronary artery bypass graft surgery on dual antiplatelet therapyPresenting symptoms:- Multiple episodes of bright red blood per rectum- Recent diarrhea (after taking bowel regimen for postoperative constipation)- No abdominal pain, rectal pain, melena, fevers, or chills\n\nPatient was vitally stable\n\n1. Laboratory Tests:- Hemoglobin: Stable- Basic metabolic panel: Stable2. Imaging Studies:- Computed tomography angiography of abdomen:  * Circumferential wall thickening of the distal rectum  * Surrounding inflammatory change  * Presacral edema  * Mucosal enhancement3. Endoscopic Findings:- Flexible sigmoidoscopy showed multiple, punched-out, irregular, nonbleeding ulcers in the rectum, measuring 2 to 6 cm4. Histopathology:- Active colitis with ulcer- Crypt distortion- Reactive changes- Viral cytopathic inclusions (highlighted by CMV immunostain)Images:- Figure 1: A 6-cm rectal ulcer with nodular base, friability, and irregular borders- Figure 2: View of rectum showing 6-cm ulcer and additional ulcerations at 3-o'clock and 9-o'clock positions- Figure 3: Microscopic views showing crypt architectural distortion, inflammatory infiltrate, and infected cells with cellular enlargement and inclusion bodies",
